WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.729
A local LLM server is a stack.

00:00:02.729 --> 00:00:06.860
Hardware provides CPU, GPU, VRAM, system memory, disk, and network capacity.

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:11.743
A runtime loads the model file, prepares prompts, manages context, and generates tokens.

00:00:11.743 --> 00:00:16.625
An API layer receives requests from chat clients, coding assistants, scripts, or agents.

00:00:16.625 --> 00:00:21.132
Load balancing works only when each layer is understood well enough to

00:00:21.132 --> 00:00:23.385
predict what a backend can handle.

00:00:23.385 --> 00:00:25.263
The model file shapes capacity.

00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:27.516
Size, format, quantization level, context capability,

00:00:27.516 --> 00:00:30.896
and runtime compatibility affect memory use and response speed.

00:00:30.896 --> 00:00:34.276
A smaller quantized model may fit on modest hardware,

00:00:34.276 --> 00:00:38.783
while a larger model may require more VRAM and longer load times.

00:00:38.783 --> 00:00:42.163
A long context setting can help with document analysis,

00:00:42.163 --> 00:00:46.294
but it can also increase prompt processing time and memory pressure.

00:00:46.294 --> 00:00:49.675
Most clients interact with local servers through HTTP APIs,

00:00:49.675 --> 00:00:54.557
often using concepts similar to cloud LLM APIs: model names, chat-style request bodies,

00:00:54.557 --> 00:00:56.435
streaming chunks, and structured errors.

00:00:56.435 --> 00:00:58.688
Compatibility is helpful but not guaranteed.

00:00:58.688 --> 00:01:03.195
Endpoint paths, request fields, headers, authentication behavior, and error formats can differ.

00:01:03.195 --> 00:01:06.951
A load balancer must preserve the behavior the client expects.

00:01:06.951 --> 00:01:08.453
Streaming is especially important.

00:01:08.453 --> 00:01:13.711
Many interfaces expect partial output while the model is still generating, so the backend,

00:01:13.711 --> 00:01:17.466
proxy, and load balancer must avoid buffering the entire response.

00:01:17.466 --> 00:01:21.222
Each backend should also be monitored as its own system.

00:01:21.222 --> 00:01:25.353
A server with a different model, smaller context window, older runtime,

00:01:25.353 --> 00:01:29.860
or weaker GPU may need different routing rules instead of equal treatment.
